Now 4 tab placeholder screens + 4 detail screens + settings.

Tab placeholders:
```tsx
import { PlaceholderScreen } from "@/components/domain/PlaceholderScreen";

export default function DemandesScreen() {
  return (
    <PlaceholderScreen
      eyebrow="Administration"
      title="Demandes"
      subtitle="Les demandes de contact envoyées depuis le site."
      phase="Phase 2"
      description="Liste, recherche, filtres, fiche détaillée, changement de statut et création de projet/client."
      icon="mail-open-outline"
    />
  );
}
```
Projets: phase 3; icon briefcase-outline.
Devis: phase 4; icon document-text-outline.
Clients: phase 3; icon people-outline.

Detail screens:
```tsx
import { useLocalSearchParams } from "expo-router";
import { PlaceholderScreen } from "@/components/domain/PlaceholderScreen";

export default function DemandeDetailScreen() {
  const { id } = useLocalSearchParams<{ id?: string }>();
  return (
    <PlaceholderScreen
      eyebrow="Demande"
      title="Fiche demande"
      subtitle={`ID : ${id ?? "—"}`}
      phase="Phase 2"
      description="Coordonnées, matériaux, message, statut et actions rapides (appeler, email, WhatsApp)."
      icon="document-text-outline"
    />
  );
}
```
Details are inside `(app)` stack with header shown, so `Screen` adds top safe area though header already exists. `SafeAreaView edges={["top"]}` inside a screen with header will add extra top padding (safe area top) under the header — on notched phones, that creates double spacing? Expo Router header already accounts for safe area; `SafeAreaView` with top edge inside the screen adds padding equal to the top inset again. Hmm. In screens with a navigation header, we shouldn't apply top edge. `PlaceholderScreen` uses `<Screen scroll>` with default edges ["top"]. For detail screens under Stack header, this adds extra top inset. Minor but not ideal.

Solution: add `edges` prop to `PlaceholderScreen` and pass `edges={[]}` for detail screens. Or modify `Screen` default to `edges={[]}`? Tabs screens have `headerShown: false` and need top safe area. Detail screens have headers.

Let me add `edges` prop:
```tsx
interface PlaceholderScreenProps { ... edges?: Edge[] }
<Screen scroll edges={edges}>
```
Default `["top"]`. Detail passes `[]`.

Similarly settings (modal header) uses `Screen scroll edges={[]}`.

Dashboard/list screens (tabs, no header) use default top. Good.

Let me update PlaceholderScreen to accept edges. Then write screens.

Settings screen:
```tsx
